by the time sons of anarchy finished its  seven season long ride back in 2014 the  fx show had become one of the must-see  crime dramas of its time it doesn't have  the nuance achieved by other critically  acclaimed shows like the wire or  breaking bad but the series lovable band  of rogues is no less entertaining sons  of anarchy follows jack's teller played  by charlie hunnam as he goes through an  existential crisis he discovers that his  long-dead father didn't want the sons of  anarchy motorcycle club redwood original  to be a criminal enterprise funded by  drugs and gun running as communicated  posthumously through a discovered  manuscript john teller's high-minded  ideals and peaceful vision for samcro  basically upends his son's entire world  jax is suddenly pulled between his duty  to his brother's conflicts with his  father's undeclared vision and what jax  himself knows to be right sons of  anarchy is a grimy and unrelenting view  of the criminal underworld but its  rogish characters are somewhat charming  however they do dish out brutal  punishments to anyone who crosses them  notably the series was envisioned as a  modern day retelling of shakespeare's  hamlet jax is the indecisive danish  prince and his father's posthumous words  are a kind of reimagined paternal ghost  i am thy father's spirit  the central conflict between jax clay  played by ron perlman and gemma played  by katie seagal even mirrors the  conflict between hamlet claudius and  gertrude from the elizabethan play  shakespeare's original story is pretty  grim in its own right but sons of  anarchy creator kurt sutter took the fx  series to some dark places even the bard  wasn't willing to conjure  gore  death and torture are splattered across  nearly every single season  since there are so many deaths  throughout the series some moments get  lost in the sea of blood however there's  one particular death from sons of  anarchy that stands above the rest  because of the emotional weight that  comes with it the death of harry opie  winston played by ryan hurst during one  of the sun's brief stints behind bars in  season 5 is emotionally devastating and  many fans are still reeling from the  loss  yes gemma killing tara knowles played by  maggie siff in season 6 is horrific  however the reason opie's death hits  harder is because it's the last  self-destructive step on a long journey  into despair that begins after opie  loses his wife in season one he's  ultimately killed in retaliation for the  death of the daughter of harold parano's  character damon pope his daughter was  accidentally killed by tig played by kim  coates when attacking another club  though he gets to play the hero and save  his brothers through self-sacrifice  opie's final act is as much an escape  for him as anything else  he's running away from a life he'd long  given up living opie volunteers to fight  in prison knowing he's going to be  killed  jacks tig and chibs played by tommy  flanagan watch on in horror as two other  inmates mercilessly beat opie to death  with a pipe to make matters even more  galling the whole thing is facilitated  by a corrections officer who's  presumably in pope's pocket that bond  between opie and the brothers he saves  is what makes it the most impactful  death in the series rolling stone voted  it the fourth best moment in sons of  anarchy overall and it was exceeded only  by sentimental scenes  no other death scene even those  involving main characters comes close  entertainment weekly also ranked opie's  death fourth in the 50 most shocking  moments of the series and his death  nabbed the top spot in a similar list at  goliath  but even after opie's gruesome slaying  sons of anarchy is clearly a series that  continues to loom large in the  imaginations of its fans the show's  enduring impact is enough to fuel  continued discussions on social media  even years after the series took its  final bow the cast were equally  heartbroken over opie's death as a way  of saying goodbye they all shaved  hurst's beard with a samurai sword  hunnam told entertainment weekly i  started crying and then mark boone  started crying by the end of it it was  the catharsis that we'd all hoped it  would be  so rip opie winston don't cry too hard  about it though ryan hurst landed a  sweet gig playing beta on the walking  dead after departing sons of anarchy and  for that we are eternally grateful check  out one of our newest videos right here  plus even more looper videos about your  favorite stuff are coming soon subscribe  to our youtube channel and hit the bell  so you don't miss a single one  you
 

